<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Resolvable Triple Arrays</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Alexey Gordeev
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Lars-Daniel Öhman
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Department of Mathematics and Mathematical Statistics, Umeå University, Sweden</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a new construction of triple arrays by combining a symmetric 2-design with a resolution of another 2-design.
This is the first general method capable of producing non-extremal triple arrays.
We call the triple arrays which can be obtained in this way <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resolvable</em>.
We employ the construction to produce the first examples of <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays, and enumerate all resolvable <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays, of which there was previously only a single known example.
An infinite subfamily of Paley triple arrays turns out to be resolvable.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We also introduce a new intermediate object, <em id="abstract1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unordered</em> triple arrays, that are to triple arrays what symmetric 2-designs are to Youden rectangles, and propose a strengthening of Agrawal’s long-standing conjecture on the existence of extremal triple arrays.
For small parameters, we completely enumerate all unordered triple arrays, and use this data to corroborate the new conjecture.
We construct several infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays, and, in particular, show that all <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays are resolvable and are in correspondence with finite affine planes of order <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">triple array</span> is an <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times c</annotation></semantics></math> array on <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> symbols that is <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">binary</span> (no repeated symbols in any row or column), <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equireplicate</span> (each symbol occurs the same number of times), and satisfies the following three intersection conditions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(RC)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any row and column have a constant number of symbols in common,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(RR)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any pair of distinct rows has a constant number of symbols in common,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(CC)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any pair of distinct columns has a constant number of symbols in common.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Triple arrays were introduced by Agrawal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">agrawalMethodsConstructionDesigns1966</span>]</cite>, though sporadic examples had appeared earlier in the literature.
For a historical overview, see the survey by Bailey <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">baileyRelationsPartitions2017</span>, Chapter 13]</cite>.
Regarding what parameter combinations are possible for triple arrays, the following theorem was shown independently using linear algebraic techniques by Bailey and Heidtmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bailey1994extremal</span>, unpublished]</cite>, Bagchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bagchiTwowayDesigns1998</span>, Corollary 1]</cite> and McSorley, Phillips, Wallis and Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mcsorleyDoubleArraysTriple2005a</span>, Theorem 3.2]</cite>.
More recently, the present authors and Markström gave an alternative purely combinatorial proof using double counting in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">gordeevTripleArrays2026</span>, Corollary 7.2(a)]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v&gt;\max(r,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v&gt;\max(r,c)</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\geq r+c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\geq r+c-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The case <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r+c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r+c-1</annotation></semantics></math> is the most well-studied, and such triple arrays are usually called <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extremal</span>.
Agrawal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">agrawalMethodsConstructionDesigns1966</span>]</cite> proposed a construction of extremal triple arrays based on a <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetric 2-design</span> and gave examples for a range of parameter sets, but it has yet to be proven that the construction always works.
Preece, Wallis and Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>]</cite> used Hadamard matrices to construct <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q\times(q+1),2q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q\times(q+1),2q)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays for all odd prime powers <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, which they named <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Paley triple arrays</span>.
Nilson and the second author <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nilsonTripleArraysYouden2015</span>]</cite> introduced a method of constructing triple arrays from <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Youden rectangles</span> and, in particular, showed that all Paley triple arrays can be constructed this way.
Nilson and Cameron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nilsonTripleArraysDifference2017</span>]</cite> further investigated this method for Youden rectangles developed from <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">difference sets</span> and constructed <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((2u^{2}-u)\times(2u^{2}+u),4u^{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((2u^{2}-u)\times(2u^{2}+u),4u^{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays for positive integers <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> with the square-free part dividing 6.
These arrays, together with Paley triple arrays, form the only two known infinite families.
Notably, all these methods can only produce extremal triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, much less is known about <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-extremal</span> triple arrays, i.e. those with <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+c-1&lt;v&lt;rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+c-1&lt;v&lt;rc</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a long while, no non-extremal examples were known.
At the British Combinatorial Conference in Aberystwyth in 1973, using different terminology, Preece asked for a <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array (also listed by Preece as an open question in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preeceNonorthogonalGraecolatinDesigns1976</span>, Table 1]</cite>), which is the smallest possible non-extremal parameter set.
An example was subsequently found in the 2000s by McSorley, Phillips, Wallis and Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mcsorleyDoubleArraysTriple2005a</span>, Section 10]</cite>, but they did not specify the details of how this design was found, and provided no analysis of its structure.
Such an analysis was, however, later given by Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">yucas_structure_7x15</span>]</cite>, who also commented that the design was originally found by computer search.
Until now, this remained the only example of a non-extremal triple array in the literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Yucas showed how to deconstruct the aforementioned <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array using two key ingredients: a <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolution</span> of the 2-design corresponding to the <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective geometry</span> <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the symmetric 2-design known as the <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fano plane</span>.
The resolution he used is one of the seven original <span id="S1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kirkman parades</span> (see Mulder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mulder1917kirkman</span>]</cite> and Cole <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">cole_kirkman_parades</span>]</cite>), and Yucas further claimed that the same construction can be carried out using other parades, which, as we will see in Section <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>, is not entirely true.
He did not explicitly mention, however, that the same idea can also in principle be applied to other compatible pairs of 2-designs, one <span id="S1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolvable</span> and one symmetric, potentially resulting in triple arrays with various parameters, both non-extremal and extremal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the present paper, we iron out the details of a general construction method based on the ideas of Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">yucas_structure_7x15</span>]</cite>, and employ it in conjunction with computational methods to produce further examples of non-extremal triple arrays, notably the first <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays.
The triple arrays produced by this method possess an interesting structure in addition to the basic properties of triple arrays. We call these triple arrays <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resolvable</em>, and enumerate completely all resolvable <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays.
It turns out that the method is also applicable for some extremal parameter sets, in particular, we show that an infinite subfamily of Paley triple arrays is resolvable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The special case of triple arrays where <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=v</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to Youden rectangles. Forgetting the order of elements in the columns of a Youden rectangle, and treating these column sets as the blocks of a block design results in a symmetric 2-design. Conversely, as proven by Smith and Hartley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">smithConstructionYoudenSquares1948</span>]</cite>, all Youden rectangles can be constructed by ordering the elements of a symmetric 2-design. For general triple arrays, we analogously introduce the notion of an <em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unordered</em> triple array, as a block design with two types of blocks, for the rows and for the columns, respectively. The corresponding ordering problem turns out to be considerably harder than in the Youden rectangle case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Agrawal’s construction mentioned above does produce unordered triple arrays from symmetric 2-designs, but it has not yet been proven that the construction results in triple arrays. The traditional formulation of the associated conjecture is that if there exists a symmetric 2-design, then there exists a triple array on the corresponding parameters. We generalize this by conjecturing that any extremal unordered triple array (except for one small counterexample) can in fact be ordered to give a triple array. Our enumerative work provides some evidence for the truth of the generalized conjecture. In the non-extremal case, the situation is different, as we find several <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays that cannot be ordered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Using finite projective geometries and Hadamard matrices, we construct three infinite series of resolvable unordered triple arrays, including a non-extremal family.
Furthermore, we show that all <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays are resolvable, and that the corresponding unordered triple arrays are in one-to-one correspondence with affine planes of order <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
We present two equivalent reformulations of the problem of constructing a triple array on these parameters, one in terms of derangements, and another in terms of multipartite hypergraphs.
The first reformulation leads to the first combinatorial explanation for the non-existence of a <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we present more formally notions from basic design theory and finite geometry, and in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we introduce unordered triple arrays. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we present Agrawal’s construction and relate it to unordered triple arrays.
Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> contains a new construction of unordered triple arrays, introduces the notion of resolvable triple arrays, and gives some concrete examples of families of arrays resulting from the construction. In Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Resolvable Paley triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we show that some Paley triple arrays are in fact resolvable. Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents our enumerative results. In Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we investigate more closely triple arrays on parameters <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>. Section <a href="#S9" title="9 Concluding remarks ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> concludes with a range of open problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>2-designs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start with the definition and basic properties of <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-designs</span>, also often referred to as <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">balanced incomplete block designs (BIBD)</span>.
For further background on designs as well as for proofs of the claims that follow, see <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em> the textbook of Beth, Jungnickel and Lenz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bethDesignTheory1999</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">block design</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> on a <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">point</span> set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a collection <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of subsets of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> called <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">blocks</span>.
It may have <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">identical blocks</span>, i.e. one set of points may appear as a block multiple times.
In other words, in general, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiset.
The points of a block design are sometimes called <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">treatments</span>, <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symbols</span>, or simply <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">elements</span>.
To avoid confusion with the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">symbols</em> used in triple arrays, we will generally use the term <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">points</span> when talking about block designs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dual</span> of a block design <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> is a block design <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}:=(\mathcal{B},P^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}:=(\mathcal{B},P^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> with the roles of points and blocks reversed: the points of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are the blocks of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and, for each point <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, the dual <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> has a block <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{*}\in P^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}\in P^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in p^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in p^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in B</annotation></semantics></math>.
When talking about dual designs, we will often make no distinction between points <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and blocks <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and treat them as one and the same.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>-design</span> (<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-design</span> for short) is a block design on a set of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> points in which each block has size <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and each pair of points occurs together in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> blocks.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The 2 in the term 2-designs refers to the <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pairs</em> mentioned in the definition.
It can be shown by double counting arguments that, setting</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r:=\frac{\lambda(v-1)}{k-1},\quad b:=\frac{vr}{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r:=\frac{\lambda(v-1)}{k-1},\quad b:=\frac{vr}{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">each point of a 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design belongs to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, and the total number of blocks is <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>.
We will refer to 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> designs as <span id="S2.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2-<math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,b,r,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,b,r,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> designs</span> when it is important to highlight all parameters.
A more involved dependency between the parameters is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fisher’s inequality)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any 2-<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,b,r,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,b,r,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design, it holds that <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq v</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The 2-designs that attain equality in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 (Fisher’s inequality). ‣ 2.1 2-designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, i.e. those with the same number of blocks and points, are called <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetric</span>.
Note that a symmetric 2-design not only has <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=v</annotation></semantics></math>, but also <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
The name <span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetric</span> comes from the fact that the dual of a symmetric 2-design is also a symmetric 2-design.
In contrast, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2 (Fisher’s inequality). ‣ 2.1 2-designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> implies that the dual of a non-symmetric 2-design cannot be a 2-design.
This duality observation can be formulated as the following theorem, giving an alternative characterization of symmetric 2-designs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In a symmetric 2-<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design, any two distinct blocks share <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> common points.
Conversely, any block design of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, each of size <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, on a set of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> points, with two distinct blocks sharing <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> common points, is a 2-<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For a block design <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> and a positive integer <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> the collection of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mb" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mb</annotation></semantics></math> blocks in which each block <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is repeated <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> times.
The <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-multiple of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the block design <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\mathcal{D}:=(P,m\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\mathcal{D}:=(P,m\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-multiple of a 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,b,r,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,b,r,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design is a 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,mb,mr,k,m\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,mb,mr,k,m\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
The <span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complement</span> of a block design <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=(P,\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math> is a block design <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathcal{D}}:=(P,\bar{\mathcal{B}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathcal{D}}:=(P,\bar{\mathcal{B}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{B}}:=\{P\setminus B\,:\,B\in\mathcal{B}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{B}}:=\{P\setminus B\,:\,B\in\mathcal{B}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The complement of a 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,b,r,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,b,r,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design is a 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,b,b-r,v-k,\lambda+b-2r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,b,b-r,v-k,\lambda+b-2r)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
In particular, the complement of a symmetric 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design is a symmetric 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,v-k,\lambda+v-2k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,v-k,\lambda+v-2k)</annotation></semantics></math> design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Looking further at the internal structure of 2-designs, in particular at the prospect of partitioning the collection of blocks in a ‘balanced’ way, the following definition is of use.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parallel class</span> in a 2-design is a set of blocks that partition the point set.
A <span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolution</span> of a 2-design is a partition of the collection of blocks into parallel classes.
A 2-design that admits a resolution is called <span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolvable</span>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a parallel class in a 2-<math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v,k,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v,k,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> design consists of <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{v}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{v}{k}</annotation></semantics></math> blocks, and a resolution has <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{bk}{v}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{bk}{v}=r</annotation></semantics></math> parallel classes.
Due to triple arrays being the main focus of the present paper, the letters <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> will from now on usually be reserved for, respectively, the number of symbols and rows in an array, and not for the parameters of a 2-design.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Finite geometries</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For integer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and a prime power <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">projective space</span> over the finite field with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> elements. The space <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math> can also be seen as a 2-design, with points and blocks of the design corresponding to points and lines of the geometry.
For integer <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG_{i}(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>PG</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG_{i}(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the 2-design with points and blocks corresponding to points and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspaces of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Similarly, let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the standard <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">affine space</span> over the finite field with <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> elements, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG_{i}(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>AG</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG_{i}(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math> the 2-design with points and blocks corresponding to points and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional affine subspaces of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> and a prime power <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(2,q)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG(2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG(2,q)</annotation></semantics></math> are called the standard projective and affine <em id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">planes</em> of order <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, or the <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Galois</span> projective and affine planes over the finite field with <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> elements.
In greater generality, any symmetric 2-<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n^{2}+n+1,n+1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n^{2}+n+1,n+1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design is usually also called a (non-standard) <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">finite projective plane of order <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and any 2-<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n^{2},n,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n^{2},n,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design is called a (non-standard) <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">finite affine plane of order <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>.
When talking about these designs, we will use the terms ‘blocks’ and ‘lines’ interchangeably.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Triple arrays and component designs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to situate triple arrays in a more general context, we state the following definition.
An <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times c</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">row-column design</span> on <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> symbols is a two-dimensional array with <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> rows and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> columns, each cell of which is filled with one of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> symbols.
It is <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">binary</span> if no symbol appears more than once in any row or column.
It is <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equireplicate</span> with <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">replication number</span> <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> if every symbol occurs <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> times in the array. With this terminology at hand, we may define triple arrays as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-triple array</span> is a binary equireplicate <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times c</annotation></semantics></math> row-column design on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> symbols in which, for some integers <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rc}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(RC)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">any row and column have <math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rc}</annotation></semantics></math> common symbols,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(RR)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">any two distinct rows have <math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}</annotation></semantics></math> common symbols,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(CC)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">any two distinct columns have <math id="S2.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math> common symbols.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The number of distinct symbols <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> used in a triple array is clearly at most the number of cells <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rc</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, since a triple array is binary, <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is at least as large as the numbers <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> of rows and columns.
Triple arrays with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r=c</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latin squares</span>, and triple arrays with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=c</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Youden rectangles</span> (also often, somewhat confusingly, called <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Youden squares</span>).
Triple arrays with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=rc</annotation></semantics></math> have a unique symbol in each cell and thus exist trivially for all <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the purposes of this paper, we generally consider only <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-trivial</span> triple arrays, i.e. those with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(r,c)&lt;v&lt;rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(r,c)&lt;v&lt;rc</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main connection between 2-designs and triple arrays is that each <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has two associated 2-designs called its <span id="S2.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">component designs</span>: the <em id="S2.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">row design</em> <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <em id="S2.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">column design</em> <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, constructed as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the symbol set of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sets of symbols appearing in row <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and column <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
Then <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of the block design <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the points of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> has a block <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}(v):=\{R_{i}\,:\,v\in R_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}(v):=\{R_{i}\,:\,v\in R_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, and condition <a href="#S2.I1.ix2" title="item (RR) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RR)</span></a> in particular, imply that <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,v,c,e,\lambda_{rr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,v,c,e,\lambda_{rr})</annotation></semantics></math> design.
Similarly, <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of the block design <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{c})</annotation></semantics></math>, has a block <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{T}(v):=\{C_{j}\,:\,v\in C_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{T}(v):=\{C_{j}\,:\,v\in C_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, and is a 2-<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,v,r,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,v,r,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Noting that the total number of cells in an <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array is <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rc</annotation></semantics></math>, its replication number must be <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\frac{rc}{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\frac{rc}{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math> are also determined by <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c,v</annotation></semantics></math> via similar double counts.
For <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rc}</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mcsorleyDoubleArraysTriple2005a</span>, Theorem 3.1]</cite>, and for <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math>, apply (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.1 2-designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to the component designs, to obtain the following identities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rc}=e=\frac{rc}{v},\quad\lambda_{rr}=\frac{c(e-1)}{r-1},\quad\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rc}=e=\frac{rc}{v},\quad\lambda_{rr}=\frac{c(e-1)}{r-1},\quad\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We see that for an <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array to exist, the parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,c,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,c,v</annotation></semantics></math> must satisfy the divisibility conditions</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rc\equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ v),\quad c(e-1)\equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ r-1),\quad r(e-1)\equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ c-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rc\equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ v),\quad c(e-1)\equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ r-1),\quad r(e-1)\equiv 0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ c-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">We will call parameter sets <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying these divisibility conditions <em id="S2.SS3.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">admissible for triple arrays</em>.
Throughout the rest of the text, unless we explicitly state otherwise, <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a parameter set admissible for triple arrays, and the corresponding parameters <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e,\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e,\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math> are given by (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Unordered triple arrays</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The component designs of a triple array are very useful in analyzing its structure, since they essentially represent properties <a href="#S2.I1.ix2" title="item (RR) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RR)</span></a> and <a href="#S2.I1.ix3" title="item (CC) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(CC)</span></a> from Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>.
However, it is not as easy to recover the property <a href="#S2.I1.ix1" title="item (RC) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RC)</span></a> just by looking at the component designs.
In light of this, we introduce the following new definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unordered triple array</span> on a set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> symbols is a collection of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> called <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">row-sets</span>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> called <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">column-sets</span> such that, for some integers <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rc}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math>, each symbol appears in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> row-sets and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> column-sets, and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{i}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rc}\text{ for all }i,j,\quad|R_{i}\cap R_{s}|=\lambda_{rr}\text{ for all }i\neq s,\quad|C_{j}\cap C_{t}|=\lambda_{cc}\text{ for all }j\neq t." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{i}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rc}\text{ for all }i,j,\quad|R_{i}\cap R_{s}|=\lambda_{rr}\text{ for all }i\neq s,\quad|C_{j}\cap C_{t}|=\lambda_{cc}\text{ for all }j\neq t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that many properties of triple arrays only depend on the contents of rows and columns and not on the exact placement of symbols in cells.
The same double counts as for triple arrays in the previous section apply for unordered triple arrays as well, and show that the parameters <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e,\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e,\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math> of an unordered triple array must likewise satisfy conditions (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
The notion of <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">admissible</span> parameters thus naturally extends to unordered triple arrays.
Similarly, we will refer to <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> as the <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">replication number</span>, call an unordered triple array <span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">extremal</span> if <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r+c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r+c-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-extremal</span> if <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+c-1&lt;v&lt;rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+c-1&lt;v&lt;rc</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A consequence of Theorem 7.3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">jagerEnumerationConstructionRowColumn2025</span>]</cite> is that for non-trivial unordered triple arrays, that is, where <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{r,c\}&lt;v&lt;rc" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{r,c\}&lt;v&lt;rc</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\neq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\neq c</annotation></semantics></math>, so the row-sets and the column-sets of a non-trivial unordered triple array actually have different sizes, and can therefore easily be distinguished from each other.
An unordered triple array <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> has two associated 2-designs, the row and column designs <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined exactly as for triple arrays, that is, <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of the block design <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{r})</annotation></semantics></math> given by the row-sets, and <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dual of the block design <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,\{C_{j}\}_{j=1}^{c})</annotation></semantics></math> given by the column-sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Any <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> immediately gives rise to an <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by treating rows and columns as unordered row-sets and column-sets.
We will call <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">underlying</span> unordered triple array of <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Clearly, the component designs of <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide.
An example of a <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4\times 9,12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4\times 9,12)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array and a representation of its underlying unordered triple array are given in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
