</figure>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">One of the main problems in the study of triple arrays is the construction of a triple array on a given set of admissible parameters.
Since any triple array <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has an underlying unordered triple array <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, one way to attack this problem is to split it into two
separate sequential problems:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an admissible parameter set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>, find an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, find an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}=U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Both these problems turn out to be difficult and, in general, do not always have any solutions.
For instances of the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with no solutions, see Examples <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Example 4.6. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Example 4.7. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.
As for the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, it has long been known, via exhaustive checking, that there are no <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays, but
a <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array is given in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We offer a novel combinatorial explanation of the non-existence of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays in Corollary <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 8.9. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.9</span></a> of Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.
In Observation <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Observation 7.7. ‣ 7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a> of Section <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>, we present more instances of the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with no solutions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
