<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>A <math id="S3.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array. Note that there are no <math id="S3.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For an <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with row-sets <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and columns-sets <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> can be viewed as a special case of the following problem, by setting <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}:=R_{i}\cap C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}:=R_{i}\cap C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a collection of finite sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq c</annotation></semantics></math>, find an <span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">array of distinct representatives</span> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}\in A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}\in A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}\neq a_{sj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}\neq a_{sj}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{ij}\neq a_{it}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{ij}\neq a_{it}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Fon-Der-Flaass <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">fon-der-flaassArraysDistinctRepresentatives1997</span>]</cite> showed that the decision version of Problem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Problem 3.4. ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is NP-complete, even if the sets <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> are restricted to have form <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}=R_{i}\cap C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}=R_{i}\cap C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for some sets <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It should be noted, though, that he proved this by reducing any instance of the problem 3-SAT to an instance of Problem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Problem 3.4. ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> in which there are sets <math id="S3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> of sizes both 2 and 3.
This does not therefore imply NP-completeness of the decision version of the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, since in this instance we have <math id="S3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A_{ij}|=|R_{i}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A_{ij}|=|R_{i}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rc}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. a constant, and, moreover, there are further restrictions on the sets <math id="S3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we explore the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> computationally by treating it as a special case of another, more well-known NP-complete problem, the <span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exact cover problem</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">To put the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> and the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> into some further context, for the trivial parameters <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r=c</annotation></semantics></math>, the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is uniquely (up to renaming of symbols) solved by setting all column-sets and row-sets to <math id="S3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is the problem of finding a <math id="S3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\times v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\times v</annotation></semantics></math> Latin square. Existence in this case is trivial, and research efforts have instead focused on other issues, such as how many different solutions there are. In the case <math id="S3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=c&gt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=c&gt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is equivalent to the problem of finding a symmetric 2-design, and the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is the problem of producing a Youden rectangle from a symmetric 2-design, which is always possible, as proven constructively by Smith and Hartley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">smithConstructionYoudenSquares1948</span>]</cite>. This goes to show that the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is explicitly solvable in at least some special cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Agrawal’s construction</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we consider the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for extremal parameter sets.
Let <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> be an extremal admissible parameter set, i.e. <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r+c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r+c-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recalling (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e-1=\frac{rc}{r+c-1}-1=\frac{(r-1)(c-1)}{v}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e-1=\frac{rc}{r+c-1}-1=\frac{(r-1)(c-1)}{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">from which we get</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}=\frac{r(r-1)}{v}=\frac{r(v-c)}{v}=r-e,\quad\text{ and, similarly, }\quad\lambda_{rr}=c-e." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext> and, similarly, </mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}=\frac{r(r-1)}{v}=\frac{r(v-c)}{v}=r-e,\quad\text{ and, similarly, }\quad\lambda_{rr}=c-e.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In 1966, Agrawal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">agrawalMethodsConstructionDesigns1966</span>]</cite> proposed a method for constructing ‘designs for two-way elimination of heterogeneity’, now known as extremal triple arrays.
Although phrased differently, the construction proceeded in essence by first giving a recipe for constructing an unordered triple array from a symmetric 2-design, as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Start with a symmetric 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on the point set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and fix one of its points <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> be the blocks of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> not containing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complements of blocks <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{R_{1}},\dots,\overline{R_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{R_{1}},\dots,\overline{R_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}:=V\setminus\overline{R_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}:=V\setminus\overline{R_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It is clear that given a symmetric 2-design, the construction can be carried out, and it is not hard to show that the resulting sets form an unordered triple array.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Taking <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> as row-sets and column-sets, respectively, gives an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Using Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3. ‣ 2.1 2-designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> and (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we have, for all <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j,s,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j,s,t</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq t</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
