<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> to the symmetric 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design with blocks</p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,2\},\{0,4,5\},\{0,3,6\},\{2,3,4\},\{1,3,5\},\{1,4,6\},\{2,5,6\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,2\},\{0,4,5\},\{0,3,6\},\{2,3,4\},\{1,3,5\},\{1,4,6\},\{2,5,6\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">that is, the Fano plane, using <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=0</annotation></semantics></math> as the fixed point, results in the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array from Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In our terminology, Agrawal thus only solved the problem of finding extremal <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unordered</em> triple arrays starting from any symmetric 2-<math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design.
In order to reach the end goal of constructing an <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array, it remains to solve the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.
Agrawal did not provide a general way of doing this, but he remarked that he was able to find a solution in all the examples he considered, provided that <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>. The following conjecture, which still remains open, was therefore implicit in Agrawal’s work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">agrawalMethodsConstructionDesigns1966</span>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If there is a symmetric 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The only parameter set excluded by the condition <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> in Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 4.4 (Agrawal []). ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, is <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>, but as seen in Example <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Example 4.3. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> the construction of an unordered triple array works in this case as well.
Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 4.4 (Agrawal []). ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> is especially important due to the fact that <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> of an unordered triple array can be reversed: let <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> be the row-sets and column-sets of an extremal <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array on a symbol set <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
Introduce a new symbol <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{R_{i}}:=V\setminus R_{i}\cup\{\sigma\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{R_{i}}:=V\setminus R_{i}\cup\{\sigma\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the blocks <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c},\overline{R_{1}},\dots,\overline{R_{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c},\overline{R_{1}},\dots,\overline{R_{r}}</annotation></semantics></math> form a symmetric 2-<math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design, which follows by repeating the arguments above in reverse.
This implies the following theorem about extremal unordered triple arrays, first shown by Bailey and Heidtmann <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bailey1994extremal</span>, unpublished]</cite> and later independently by McSorley, Phillips, Wallis and Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mcsorleyDoubleArraysTriple2005a</span>, Theorem 5.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array can be obtained via <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.
In particular, if there is an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-(unordered) triple array, then there is a symmetric 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> essentially reduce the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> in the extremal case to the widely studied, though in general still very much open question of existence of symmetric 2-designs.
In particular, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> implies non-existence of extremal (unordered) triple arrays when non-existence of the corresponding symmetric 2-designs is known.
The following two examples were given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mcsorleyDoubleArraysTriple2005a</span>, Section 7]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even though the parameter set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,21)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,21)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible for triple arrays, there are no symmetric 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(22,7,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(22,7,2)</annotation></semantics></math> designs, and thus, by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, no <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,21)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>21</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,21)</annotation></semantics></math>-(unordered) triple arrays.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, the parameter set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15\times 28,42)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>42</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15\times 28,42)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible for triple arrays, and, moreover, there are known examples of would-be component designs, i.e. 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,10,18)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,10,18)</annotation></semantics></math> and 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(28,10,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(28,10,5)</annotation></semantics></math> designs.
However, there are no symmetric 2-<math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(43,15,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>43</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(43,15,5)</annotation></semantics></math> designs and thus no <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15\times 28,42)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>42</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15\times 28,42)</annotation></semantics></math>-(unordered) triple arrays.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Since any triple array has an underlying unordered triple array, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> in turn implies that any extremal triple array can be obtained from <em id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">some</em> unordered triple array obtained from Agrawal’s construction. It may, however, be the case that some extremal unordered triple arrays cannot be ordered.
The following strengthening of Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 4.4 (Agrawal []). ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> therefore seems to better reflect Agrawal’s statement that ‘‘such rearrangement is always possible’’.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}=U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Triple arrays via resolutions</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section contains our main contribution, namely the first general method of solving the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for some non-extremal parameter sets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Resolvable triple arrays</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">yucas_structure_7x15</span>]</cite> provided an analysis of the internal
structure of a particular <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array, by examining its component designs.
The array in question is given in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, together with a representation of its underlying unordered triple array which highlights its internal structure: the symbols are partitioned into 7 groups of 5 symbols, with symbols in one group all appearing together in the same row-sets, and each column-set containing precisely one symbol from each group.
To make this into a general construction, the analysis has to be reversed, as follows.</p>
