<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_construction">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> be a parameter set admissible for triple arrays such that, in addition to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e,\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e,\lambda_{rc},\lambda_{rr},\lambda_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following two parameters are integers:</span></p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}:=\frac{e(e-1)}{r-1}\in{\mathbb{Z}},\quad k:=\frac{c}{e}\in{\mathbb{Z}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}:=\frac{e(e-1)}{r-1}\in{\mathbb{Z}},\quad k:=\frac{c}{e}\in{\mathbb{Z}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Start with a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,r,e,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,r,e,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on the point set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,r\}</annotation></semantics></math> with blocks labeled <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\dots,S_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\dots,S_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a resolution <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,v,r,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,v,r,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design on the point set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,c\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,c\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote the parallel classes of the resolution by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{B}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{B}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote the blocks comprising a parallel class by <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{x}=\{B_{x1},\dots,B_{xk}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{x}=\{B_{x1},\dots,B_{xk}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Treating the blocks <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> as symbols, define row-sets <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and column-sets <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{i}:=\bigcup_{x\,:\,i\in S_{x}}\mathcal{B}_{x},\quad C_{j}:=\{B_{xy}\ :\ j\in B_{xy}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}:=\bigcup_{x\,:\,i\in S_{x}}\mathcal{B}_{x},\quad C_{j}:=\{B_{xy}\ :\ j\in B_{xy}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, it is clear that the construction can be carried out, given a symmetric 2-design and a resolvable 2-design.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The sets <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> form an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Indeed, by definition, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{i}|=ek=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{i}|=ek=c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{j}|=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{j}|=r</annotation></semantics></math>, each symbol <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{xy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{xy}</annotation></semantics></math> appears in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> row-sets and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> column-sets, and, for all <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq s</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|C_{j}\cap C_{t}|=\lambda_{cc},\quad|R_{i}\cap R_{s}|=\sum_{x\,:\,\{i,s\}\subseteq S_{x}}|\mathcal{B}_{x}|=\lambda_{rrc}k=\frac{(e-1)c}{r-1}=\lambda_{rr}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{j}\cap C_{t}|=\lambda_{cc},\quad|R_{i}\cap R_{s}|=\sum_{x\,:\,\{i,s\}\subseteq S_{x}}|\mathcal{B}_{x}|=\lambda_{rrc}k=\frac{(e-1)c}{r-1}=\lambda_{rr}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, for each parallel class <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{B}_{x}\cap C_{j}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{B}_{x}\cap C_{j}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, thus, for all <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|R_{i}\cap C_{j}|=\sum_{x\,:\,i\in S_{x}}|\mathcal{B}_{x}\cap C_{j}|=e=\lambda_{rc}.\qed" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{i}\cap C_{j}|=\sum_{x\,:\,i\in S_{x}}|\mathcal{B}_{x}\cap C_{j}|=e=\lambda_{rc}.\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of notation for the parameter <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> is explained in Section <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Quad arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
Note that the column design <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the row design <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-multiple of <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
More precisely, for each <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq x\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq x\leq r</annotation></semantics></math>, the blocks <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}(B_{x1}),\dots,CD_{U}(B_{xk})\in\mathcal{B}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}(B_{x1}),\dots,CD_{U}(B_{xk})\in\mathcal{B}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> form a parallel class in <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the blocks <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{U}(B_{x1}),\dots,RD_{U}(B_{xk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{U}(B_{x1}),\dots,RD_{U}(B_{xk})</annotation></semantics></math> are identical, each corresponding to the block <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This motivates the following definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array is <span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolvable</span> if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> defined as in (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In Construction 5.1 (RUTA construction). ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) are integers, and the symbols can be partitioned into <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> groups of size <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, with each column-set containing precisely one symbol from each group, and symbols in one group all appearing in the same row-sets.
An <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array is <span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolvable</span> if its underlying unordered triple array is resolvable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A parameter set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">admissible for resolvable triple arrays</span> if it is admissible for triple arrays and, additionally, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are integers.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any unordered triple array obtained from the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is clearly resolvable.
On the other hand, for any resolvable unordered triple array <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, its row design <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-multiple of some symmetric 2-<math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{T}=k\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{T}=k\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the groups of symbols induce a resolution <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> on its column design <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> can be obtained by using the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with these <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The unordered triple arrays from Figures <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are both resolvable.
Alternative representations with symbols rearranged to highlight their structure are given in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
