<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Not all (unordered) triple arrays are resolvable, even when the corresponding <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are integers.
Indeed, for the parameter set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 8,14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 8,14)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and two examples of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 8,14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 8,14)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays, one resolvable and one not, are given in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
