<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Although the transpose of an (unordered) triple array is always an (unordered) triple array, the next lemma shows that it can only be resolvable with regards to one orientation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If a non-trivial parameter set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible for resolvable triple arrays, then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c\times r,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c\times r,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is not admissible for resolvable triple arrays.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume, for a contradiction, that <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c\times r,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c\times r,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible. Then <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> must divide <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-1</annotation></semantics></math> is coprime with <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=\frac{e(e-1)}{r-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=\frac{e(e-1)}{r-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\leq\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\leq\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, since <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, and equivalently <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e-1&lt;r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e-1&lt;r-1</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S5.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=\frac{e(e-1)}{r-1}&lt;e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=\frac{e(e-1)}{r-1}&lt;e</annotation></semantics></math>, a contradiction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Quad arrays</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Resolvable (unordered) triple arrays turn out to have an interesting additional intersection property.
Returning to the notation from the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, for all <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|R_{i}\cap R_{s}\cap C_{j}|=\sum_{x\,:\,\{i,s\}\subseteq S_{x}}|\mathcal{B}_{x}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rrc}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{i}\cap R_{s}\cap C_{j}|=\sum_{x\,:\,\{i,s\}\subseteq S_{x}}|\mathcal{B}_{x}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rrc}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This motivates the following definitions, where we note that the parameter <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> can be directly calculated from the parameters <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S5.E4" title="In Construction 5.1 (RUTA construction). ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-unordered quad array</span> is an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array with row-sets <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and column-sets <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which, for some integer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{i}\cap R_{s}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rrc}\text{ for all }i\neq s\text{ and }j." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{i}\cap R_{s}\cap C_{j}|=\lambda_{rrc}\text{ for all }i\neq s\text{ and }j.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-quad array</span> is an <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array satisfying, for some integer <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math>, an additional property:</p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(RRC)</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">any triple of two distinct rows and a column shares <math id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> common symbols.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A parameter set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">admissible for quad arrays</span> if it is admissible for triple arrays and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The name ‘quad array’ continues the established naming convention: recalling Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, a triple array is a binary equireplicate row-column design that satisfies three intersection properties <a href="#S2.I1.ix1" title="item (RC) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RC)</span></a>, <a href="#S2.I1.ix2" title="item (RR) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RR)</span></a> and <a href="#S2.I1.ix3" title="item (CC) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(CC)</span></a>.
Similarly, a binary equireplicate row-column design is called a <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mono array</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">jagerEnumerationConstructionRowColumn2025</span>, Definition 2.4]</cite> if it satisfies <a href="#S2.I1.ix3" title="item (CC) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(CC)</span></a>, and a <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">double array</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mcsorleyDoubleArraysTriple2005a</span>, Section 2]</cite> if it satisfies <a href="#S2.I1.ix2" title="item (RR) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RR)</span></a> and <a href="#S2.I1.ix3" title="item (CC) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(CC)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.9</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Not all triple arrays are quad arrays, even when <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer, as evidenced by the examples in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
The top triple array is resolvable and is therefore a quad array.
On the other hand, in the bottom triple array, for instance, <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{1}\cap R_{2}\cap C_{6}|=|\{14\}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{1}\cap R_{2}\cap C_{6}|=|\{14\}|=1</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S5.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R_{1}\cap R_{2}\cap C_{7}|=|\{4,13,14\}|=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R_{1}\cap R_{2}\cap C_{7}|=|\{4,13,14\}|=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.10</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted above, any resolvable (unordered) triple array is an (unordered) quad array.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, we were not able to find an (unordered) quad array which is not resolvable.
This is somewhat surprising as the definition of a quad array does not require that <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\frac{c}{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\frac{c}{e}</annotation></semantics></math> is an integer, and even when this is the case, there is no obvious reason why every quad array should be resolvable.
For example, the parameter set <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(16\times 9,24)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(16\times 9,24)</annotation></semantics></math> is admissible for quad arrays but not for resolvable triple arrays.
However, in Observation <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem4" title="Observation 7.4. ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a> of Section <a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a> we will see that there are <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(16\times 9,24)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(16\times 9,24)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays but no <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(16\times 9,24)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(16\times 9,24)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered quad arrays.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we use the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> to produce three infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays, two extremal and one non-extremal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.11</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any prime power <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a resolvable <math id="S5.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{q^{n}-1}{q-1}\times q^{n},\frac{q(q^{n}-1)}{q-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{q^{n}-1}{q-1}\times q^{n},\frac{q(q^{n}-1)}{q-1})</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The corresponding parameters are <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=q^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=q^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=\frac{q^{n-1}-1}{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=\frac{q^{n-1}-1}{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}=q^{n-1}(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}=q^{n-1}(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=q^{n-2}(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=q^{n-2}(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=q</annotation></semantics></math>.
When <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the trivial symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1,q,q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1,q,q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
When <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG_{n-2}(n-1,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>PG</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG_{n-2}(n-1,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{q^{n}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{n-1}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{n-2}-1}{q-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{q^{n}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{n-1}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{n-2}-1}{q-1})</annotation></semantics></math> design, so let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be its complement, that is, a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{q^{n}-1}{q-1},q^{n-1},q^{n-2}(q-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{q^{n}-1}{q-1},q^{n-1},q^{n-2}(q-1))</annotation></semantics></math> design.
In any case, <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> has parameters <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unique resolution of the 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG_{n-1}(n,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>AG</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG_{n-1}(n,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The theorem follows by applying the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with these <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We have already seen in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> the first two unordered triple arrays from this family, corresponding to <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.12</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, <em id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> affine plane of order <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to a resolvable <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array: apply the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with the trivial symmetric 2-<math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1,q,q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1,q,q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design as <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and the unique resolution of this affine plane as <math id="S5.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The current state of knowledge is that there are non-Galois affine planes for some prime power orders, but no known such planes for prime orders.
Also, there are no known affine planes for non-prime-power orders, but the construction presented here would in principle work for any finite affine plane.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we further investigate parameters sets corresponding to Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Remark 5.12. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a> and the case <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>.
In particular, in Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 8.3. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a> we show that all triple arrays on such parameter sets are resolvable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.13</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If there is a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4m-1,2m-1,m-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4m-1,2m-1,m-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design, then there is a resolvable <math id="S5.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((4m-1)\times 4m,8m-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((4m-1)\times 4m,8m-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The corresponding parameters are <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=2m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=2m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=2m-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}=2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}=2m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> be a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4m-1,2m-1,m-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4m-1,2m-1,m-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design on the symbol set <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with blocks <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},\dots,A_{4m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},\dots,A_{4m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> the 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,e,\lambda_{rr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,e,\lambda_{rr})</annotation></semantics></math> design on the symbol set <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\cup\{\sigma\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\cup\{\sigma\}</annotation></semantics></math> with blocks</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}\cup\{\sigma\},\ V\setminus A_{1},\ A_{2}\cup\{\sigma\},\ V\setminus A_{2},\ \dots,\ A_{4m-1}\cup\{\sigma\},\ V\setminus A_{4m-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}\cup\{\sigma\},\ V\setminus A_{1},\ A_{2}\cup\{\sigma\},\ V\setminus A_{2},\ \dots,\ A_{4m-1}\cup\{\sigma\},\ V\setminus A_{4m-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a resolution <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> with parallel classes <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{A_{i}\cup\{\sigma\},V\setminus A_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{A_{i}\cup\{\sigma\},V\setminus A_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, let <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complement of <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math> design.
The theorem follows by applying Construction <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Construction 5.1 (RUTA construction). ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> with these <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The symmetric designs used in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> belong to a well-known class of designs based on Hadamard matrices.
An <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hadamard matrix of order <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math></span> is an <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pm 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pm 1)</annotation></semantics></math>-matrix with pairwise orthogonal rows.
For a general overview on Hadamard matrices as well as for further details on the facts listed below, see the recent book by Seberry and Yamada <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">seberryHadamardMatricesConstructions2020</span>]</cite>.
It is known that a symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4m-1,2m-1,m-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4m-1,2m-1,m-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design exists if and only if there exists an Hadamard matrix of order <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4m</annotation></semantics></math>.
The long-standing <span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hadamard conjecture</span> states that Hadamard matrices of order <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4m</annotation></semantics></math> exist for all <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
The smallest order for which no Hadamard matrix is known is <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="668" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>668</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">668</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to <math id="S5.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=167" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>167</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=167</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives the following corollary to Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.14</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq m&lt;167" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>167</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq m&lt;167</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a resolvable <math id="S5.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((4m-1)\times 4m,8m-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((4m-1)\times 4m,8m-2)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Paley <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">paleyOrthogonalMatrices1933</span>]</cite> constructed two infinite series of Hadamard matrices, one with orders <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+1</annotation></semantics></math> for prime powers <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, and one with orders <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math> for prime powers <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.15</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any prime power <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a resolvable <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q\times(q+1),2q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q\times(q+1),2q)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.
For any prime power <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 1\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a resolvable <math id="S5.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((2q+1)\times(2q+2),4q+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((2q+1)\times(2q+2),4q+2)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 6.1. ‣ 6 Resolvable Paley triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> of Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Resolvable Paley triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we show that for any prime power <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math> there in fact exists a resolvable <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q\times(q+1),2q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q\times(q+1),2q)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Curiously, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>, Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Remark 5.12. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> seem to cover most extremal parameter sets admissible for resolvable triple arrays, see Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The smallest extremal parameter set not covered by them and for which the existence of unordered triple arrays is not ruled out by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> and the non-existence of symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> designs, is <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(37\times 112,148)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>37</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>112,148</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(37\times 112,148)</annotation></semantics></math>.
It should be noted that there are no known examples of symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(149,37,9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>149</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(149,37,9)</annotation></semantics></math> designs, corresponding to <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, nor of resolvable 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(112,28,9)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>112</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(112,28,9)</annotation></semantics></math> designs, corresponding to the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
