<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The list of all extremal parameter sets with <math id="S5.T1.m210" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.T1.m211" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq c</annotation></semantics></math> admissible for resolvable triple arrays, excluding those where there are no symmetric 2-<math id="S5.T1.m212" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> designs and thus, by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, no unordered triple arrays.
When the parameter set is covered by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a>, Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Remark 5.12. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a> or Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a>, the corresponding column lists the relevant parameter values.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">So far, all the families of unordered triple arrays presented have been extremal, that is, with <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=r+c-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=r+c-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Importantly, the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> also works for some non-extremal parameter sets, as proven in the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem16" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.16</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any prime power <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem16.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{q^{3}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{4}-1}{q-1},\frac{(q^{4}-1)(q^{3}-1)}{(q^{2}-1)(q-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{q^{3}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{4}-1}{q-1},\frac{(q^{4}-1)(q^{3}-1)}{(q^{2}-1)(q-1)})</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The corresponding parameters are <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=q+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}=q^{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}=q^{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=q^{2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=q^{2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denniston <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">DennistonPackings</span>]</cite>, and later Beutelspacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BeutelspacherParallelisms</span>]</cite> proved that <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is resolvable for any prime power <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
The theorem follows by applying the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with the symmetric 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}:=\PG(2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}:=\PG(2,q)</annotation></semantics></math> and a resolution <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of the 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S5.SS3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 5.16. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> gives <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays, an example of which is given in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
A more detailed investigation of this case is presented in Section <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>, where we enumerate and analyse the structure of all resolvable <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-(unordered) triple arrays.
For <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting unordered triple array has parameters <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>, for which there is no previously known example, as is also the case for larger <math id="S5.SS3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> lists all non-extremal parameter sets with <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 30</annotation></semantics></math> admissible for resolvable triple arrays.
Besides the parameter sets covered by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 5.16. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math> is the only non-extremal parameter set <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> with known examples of resolvable 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> designs.
A number of resolutions of 2-<math id="S5.SS3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,5,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,5,6)</annotation></semantics></math> designs have been constructed by Mathon and Rosa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mathon15FamilyBIBDs1989</span>, Table 6]</cite>, and in Section <a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5 Resolvable (21×15,63)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a> we use these resolutions to find <math id="S5.SS3.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays, no examples of which were known before.</p>
</div>
