<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Resolvable Paley triple arrays</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As we have seen, the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> provides a novel way of solving the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for a number of parameter sets.
In particular, it is the first general method of doing this for non-extremal parameter sets.
Nevertheless, similar to <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, it gives no indication of how to then solve the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.
There are two known infinite families of triple arrays <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nilsonTripleArraysDifference2017</span>]</cite>, both of which are extremal.
They can each be seen as an implicit way of solving the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for certain parameter sets, though it should be mentioned that in both cases the triple array is constructed directly, without an intermediate step of building an unordered triple array.
In this section we investigate these two families and show that one of them contains an infinite number of resolvable triple arrays as a subfamily, while the other family contains no resolvable triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Preece, Wallis and Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>]</cite> used the Hadamard matrices constructed by Paley in a manner somewhat different from Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 5.13. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.13</span></a> to construct an infinite family of <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q\times(q+1),2q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q\times(q+1),2q)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays for all odd prime powers <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, which they called Paley triple arrays.
When <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, their construction gives <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> different types of arrays, and examining them closer, we can show that the first four of these types are resolvable, giving an infinite family of resolvable triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To prepare for this result, we follow notation used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>, p.241]</cite>, and let <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}=\{0=w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{q}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}=\{0=w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{q}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}^{\prime}=\{0^{\prime}=w_{1}^{\prime},w_{2}^{\prime},\dots,w_{q}^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>0</mn><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}^{\prime}=\{0^{\prime}=w_{1}^{\prime},w_{2}^{\prime},\dots,w_{q}^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be two duplicate copies of the finite field with <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> elements.
Let <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of non-zero squares of elements of <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of non-squares, and let <math id="S6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{0}:=Q\cup\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{0}:=Q\cup\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{0}:=N\cup\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{0}:=N\cup\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For two non-zero elements <math id="S6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b\in F_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b\in F_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the <math id="S6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\times(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\times(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math> array <math id="S6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(i,j)=\begin{cases}w_{i}-\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{a}&amp;\text{if }j\leq q\text{ and }w_{i}-w_{j}\in Q,\\
(w_{i}+\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{b})^{\prime}&amp;\text{if }j\leq q\text{ and }w_{i}-w_{j}\in N_{0},\\
w_{i}&amp;\text{if }j=q+1.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(i,j)=\begin{cases}w_{i}-\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{a}&amp;\text{if }j\leq q\text{ and }w_{i}-w_{j}\in Q,\\
(w_{i}+\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{b})^{\prime}&amp;\text{if }j\leq q\text{ and }w_{i}-w_{j}\in N_{0},\\
w_{i}&amp;\text{if }j=q+1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Assuming that <math id="S6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)(b+1)\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)(b+1)\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>, and that if <math id="S6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-1\in N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-1\in N</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>, it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>, Theorem 10]</cite> that <math id="S6.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a triple array.
We will assume that both <math id="S6.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)(b+1)\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)(b+1)\in Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>. This corresponds to Paley triple arrays of types 1-4 out of the 6 types given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>, proof of Theorem 10]</cite>, which, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">preecePaleyTripleArrays2005</span>, Corollary 12]</cite> all exist for every <math id="S6.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any prime power <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, Paley <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q\times(q+1),2q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q\times(q+1),2q)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array of types 1–4 are resolvable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Partition <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}\cup{\mathbb{F}}_{q}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}\cup{\mathbb{F}}_{q}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> pairs <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{s},w_{s}^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{s},w_{s}^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s\leq q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s\leq q</annotation></semantics></math>.
To prove that a Paley triple array <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is resolvable, we will show that, for each <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the symbols <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> appear in the same rows, and each column contains precisely one of <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s},w_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s},w_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Denote by <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> the sets of symbols appearing in row <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and column <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
By definition, <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}=(w_{i}-\frac{Q_{0}}{a})\cup(w_{i}+\frac{N_{0}}{b})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=(w_{i}-\frac{Q_{0}}{a})\cup(w_{i}+\frac{N_{0}}{b})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and since <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-square in <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{F}}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔽</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{F}}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S6.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv 3\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 4)</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that <math id="S6.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{i}-\frac{Q_{0}}{a})=(w_{i}+\frac{N_{0}}{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{i}-\frac{Q_{0}}{a})=(w_{i}+\frac{N_{0}}{a})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S6.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>, either both <math id="S6.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> are square, or they are both non-square.
We therefore have either <math id="S6.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}=(w_{i}+N_{0})\cup(w_{i}+N_{0})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=(w_{i}+N_{0})\cup(w_{i}+N_{0})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}=(w_{i}+Q_{0})\cup(w_{i}+Q_{0})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=(w_{i}+Q_{0})\cup(w_{i}+Q_{0})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In both cases, for each <math id="S6.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, the symbols <math id="S6.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> either both occur, or both do not occur in <math id="S6.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, using <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}-\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{a}=w_{j}+\frac{a-1}{a}(w_{i}-w_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}-\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{a}=w_{j}+\frac{a-1}{a}(w_{i}-w_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{i}+\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{b})^{\prime}=(w_{j}+\frac{b+1}{b}(w_{i}-w_{j}))^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{i}+\frac{w_{i}-w_{j}}{b})^{\prime}=(w_{j}+\frac{b+1}{b}(w_{i}-w_{j}))^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}=(w_{j}+\frac{a-1}{a}Q)\cup(w_{j}+\frac{b+1}{b}N_{0})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}=(w_{j}+\frac{a-1}{a}Q)\cup(w_{j}+\frac{b+1}{b}N_{0})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ab\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ab\in Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)(b+1)\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)(b+1)\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we have either <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}=(w_{j}+Q)\cup(w_{j}+N_{0})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}=(w_{j}+Q)\cup(w_{j}+N_{0})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}=(w_{j}+N)\cup(w_{j}+Q_{0})^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}=(w_{j}+N)\cup(w_{j}+Q_{0})^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In any case, <math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> contains exactly one of <math id="S6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s},w_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s},w_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Nilson and Cameron <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nilsonTripleArraysDifference2017</span>, Theorem 4.8]</cite> constructed <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((2u^{2}-u)\times(2u^{2}+u),4u^{2}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((2u^{2}-u)\times(2u^{2}+u),4u^{2}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays for all positive integers <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> with the square-free part dividing 6.
The corresponding parameter <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=u^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=u^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> divides neither <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=2u^{2}-u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=2u^{2}-u</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=2u^{2}+u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=2u^{2}+u</annotation></semantics></math>, so these arrays are never resolvable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Enumeration and examples</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first briefly describe the notions of equivalence we used when enumerating various objects.
We then give an overview over the enumerative problems treated, how we have handled them algorithmically, and our results.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Notions of equivalence</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isomorphism</span> between two block designs is a pair of bijective maps, one between their point sets and one between their block collections, which preserve the point-block inclusion relationship.
An <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isomorphism</span> between two resolutions of 2-designs is an isomorphism between these 2-designs which maps the parallel classes of one resolution to the parallel classes of the other.
An <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isomorphism</span> between two unordered triple arrays consists of three bijective maps, between their symbol sets, collections of row-sets and collections of column-sets, which preserve the inclusion relationships.
Two objects are <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isomorphic</span> if there exists an isomorphism between them.
An <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">automorphism</span> is an isomorphism of the object with itself.
All automorphisms of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> form its <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(full) automorphism group</span> <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that a 2-design may have multiple non-isomorphic resolutions.
The automorphism group of a resolution <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-design <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of the automorphism group of the 2-design: <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut\mathcal{R}\leq\Aut\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut\mathcal{R}\leq\Aut\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two triple arrays <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">isotopic</span> if there exist bijective maps <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{R},\varphi_{C},\varphi_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{R},\varphi_{C},\varphi_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> between their sets of rows, sets of columns, and symbol sets, respectively, such that if the cell <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains symbol <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, then the cell <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{R}(i),\varphi_{C}(j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{R}(i),\varphi_{C}(j))</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> contains symbol <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{V}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{V}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">autotopism group</span> <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut T</annotation></semantics></math> of a triple array <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> consists of all its <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">autotopisms</span>, that is, isotopisms of <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with itself.
If two triple arrays <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are isotopic, then their underlying unordered triple arrays <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> are isomorphic.
On the other hand, an unordered triple array can, in general, be ordered in multiple non-isotopic ways, see Figure <a href="#S7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 7.1 Notions of equivalence ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
For a triple array <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut T\leq\Aut U_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut T\leq\Aut U_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
