<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Two examples of non-isotopic <math id="S7.F6.m95" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5\times 6,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5\times 6,10)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays sharing the same underlying unordered triple array.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can therefore define an equivalence relation on triple arrays based on whether they have the same underlying unordered triple array.
For some further context on this relation, an <em id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intercalate</em> in a triple array is a <math id="S7.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 2</annotation></semantics></math> subsquare on two symbols, and flipping an intercalate means exchanging positions of the two symbols involved. This operation does not change the contents of any row-set or column-set, and so does not change the underlying unordered triple array. More generally, a <em id="S7.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trade</em> in a triple array is a set of cells such that there is a rearrangement of the symbols in these cells such that the row-sets and the column-sets do not change. If two triple arrays share the same underlying unordered triple array, they may be seen as both having trivial trades, carrying the one to the other by rearranging symbols while still leaving row-sets and column-sets intact.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Computational methods</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we briefly describe how we approach the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> and the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> computationally.
In all cases, we use the package nauty by McKay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mckayPracticalGraphIsomorphism2014</span>]</cite> to detect isomorphisms/isotopisms and calculate automorphism/autotopism group sizes.
All algorithms described below were implemented in C++ and run on a personal laptop.
The total run time for all cases was around a hundred core hours.
The source code and all generated data are available at <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">gordeevZenodoLibrary25</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Ordering unordered triple arrays</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to search for solutions to the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, we restate it as a special case of the following well-known NP-complete problem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact Cover Problem)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a set <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and a collection of its subsets <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, find an <span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exact cover</span> of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, a subcollection <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{*}\subseteq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of pairwise disjoint subsets such that <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigcup_{A\in\mathcal{A}^{*}}A=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigcup_{A\in\mathcal{A}^{*}}A=X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For an <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with row-sets <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and columns-sets <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is a special instance of the <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="In Ordering unordered triple arrays ‣ 7.2 Computational methods ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 7.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact Cover Problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with</p>
<table id="S7.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X:=\{x_{ij}\,:\,1\leq i\leq r,1\leq j\leq c\}\cup\{y_{ia}\,:\,1\leq i\leq r,a\in R_{i}\}\cup\{z_{ja}\,:\,1\leq j\leq c,a\in C_{j}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:=\{x_{ij}\,:\,1\leq i\leq r,1\leq j\leq c\}\cup\{y_{ia}\,:\,1\leq i\leq r,a\in R_{i}\}\cup\{z_{ja}\,:\,1\leq j\leq c,a\in C_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S7.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{A}:=\{\{x_{ij},y_{ia},z_{ja}\}\,:\,1\leq i\leq r,1\leq j\leq c,a\in R_{i}\cap C_{j}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}:=\{\{x_{ij},y_{ia},z_{ja}\}\,:\,1\leq i\leq r,1\leq j\leq c,a\in R_{i}\cap C_{j}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dancing links</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">knuthDancingLinks2000</span>]</cite> and <span id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dancing cells</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">knuth2025fascicle7</span>, Section ‘‘Dancing cells’’]</cite> backtracking algorithms provide a standard and quite efficient approach to the <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="In Ordering unordered triple arrays ‣ 7.2 Computational methods ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 7.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact Cover Problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.
The special case we are interested in has a number of properties which can be used to streamline and speed up the algorithms. For example, each element of <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to at most <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> subsets from the collection <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, the replication number of <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, is quite small compared to the total size of <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, each set <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> has constant size 3, much smaller than the total size of <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
To fully utilize these properties and maximize the search efficiency, we have implemented our own specialized version of the Dancing cells algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a given unordered triple array <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find all non-isotopic triple arrays <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}=U</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: first, find all solutions to the corresponding instance of the <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="In Ordering unordered triple arrays ‣ 7.2 Computational methods ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 7.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact Cover Problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> using the Dancing cells algorithm, and then remove isotopic copies from the resulting list of triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To verify the correctness of our computations, we perform a consistency check based on the orbit-stabilizer theorem: for each found triple array <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, one must have <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Aut T|\cdot N(T)=|\Aut U|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Aut T|\cdot N(T)=|\Aut U|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of found triple arrays isotopic to <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
We verify that this identity holds for each triple array <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> obtained during the search.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In some cases where enumerating all solutions to the <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem1" title="In Ordering unordered triple arrays ‣ 7.2 Computational methods ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 7.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exact Cover Problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is not feasible, usually due to space constraints, we use the Dancing cells algorithm to only look for examples of solutions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Enumerating extremal unordered triple arrays</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If two <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays are isomorphic, they correspond to isomorphic symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> designs in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.
Therefore, if the complete list of non-isomorphic symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> designs is known, then, by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, we can generate all non-isomorphic <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays as follows: for each symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on the point set <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and each point <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, apply <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> to obtain the unordered triple array <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then remove isomorphic copies from the resulting list of unordered triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To verify the correctness of our computations, we again perform a consistency check based on the orbit-stabilizer theorem: for each <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> we verify that <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Aut U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}|\cdot N(U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma})=|\Aut\mathcal{S}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Aut U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}|\cdot N(U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma})=|\Aut\mathcal{S}|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of constructed unordered triple arrays isomorphic to <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{S},\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Enumerating resolvable unordered triple arrays</h4>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generate all non-isomorphic resolvable <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays corresponding to a given symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with blocks <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\dots,S_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\dots,S_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and a given resolution <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-<math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design with parallel classes <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{Z}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{Z}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each permutation <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in\mathfrak{S}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in\mathfrak{S}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with parallel classes labeled <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{i}:=\mathcal{Z}_{\pi(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{i}:=\mathcal{Z}_{\pi(i)}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the unordered triple array <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then remove isomorphic copies from the resulting list of unordered triple arrays.
Note that the choice of <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> was implicit in the description of the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> as the parallel classes were already assumed to be labeled, but different permutations <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> may lead to non-isomorphic unordered triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to previous cases, we verify the correctness of our computations by performing a consistency check based on the orbit-stabilizer theorem: for each <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> we verify that <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Aut U_{\pi}|\cdot N(U_{\pi})=|\Aut\mathcal{S}|\cdot|\Aut\mathcal{R}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Aut U_{\pi}|\cdot N(U_{\pi})=|\Aut\mathcal{S}|\cdot|\Aut\mathcal{R}|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(U_{\pi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(U_{\pi})</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of constructed unordered triple arrays isomorphic to <math id="S7.SS2.SSSx3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>The extremal case</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Heinlein, Ivanov, McKay and Östergård <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">heinlein2023</span>]</cite> provide complete lists of non-isomorphic symmetric 2-designs for a number of parameters.
Using this data, we employ <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> to generate all corresponding unordered triple arrays.
We excluded symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(31,15,7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>31</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(31,15,7)</annotation></semantics></math> designs from consideration, as their total number exceeds 10 billion, and only some are given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">heinlein2023</span>]</cite>.
On the other hand, to cover all known symmetric 2-designs with <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=2</annotation></semantics></math>, also known as <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">biplanes</span>, we applied <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> to the two symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(79,13,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>79</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(79,13,2)</annotation></semantics></math> designs produced by SageMath <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">sagemath25</span>]</cite>, which were first constructed by Aschbacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">aschbacherCollineationGroupsSymmetric1971</span>]</cite>.
To the best of our knowledge, full enumerations of symmetric 2-designs are not known for any parameters not present in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">heinlein2023</span>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For some parameter sets, we use the obtained complete lists of non-isomorphic unordered triple arrays to generate all non-isotopic triple arrays.
This gives a further correctness check, as we verify that the number of found triple arrays matches our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">gordeevTripleArrays2026</span>, Table C.1]</cite>, where a completely different algorithm was used.
For larger parameter sets, the enumeration of all non-isotopic triple arrays turned out to be unattainable with current methods, mainly due to space limitations.
In each such case, we empirically investigate Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> by finding at least one triple array for each unordered triple array.
The summary of results is given in Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
For more details, see Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Counts of extremal (unordered) triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
