<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every unordered triple array in Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> corresponds to at least one triple array, which corroborates Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Having generated all unordered triple arrays, and in some cases all triple arrays, we can also easily check which of them are resolvable and which of them are (unordered) quad arrays.
The summary is given in Table <a href="#S7.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
