<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>The number of non-isomorphic unordered triple arrays (UTA), unordered quad arrays (UQA) and resolvable unordered triple arrays (RUTA), non-isotopic triple arrays (TA), quad arrays (QA) and resolvable triple arrays (RTA).
The parameter sets covered here are those from Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> with integer <math id="S7.T4.m107" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S7.T4.m108" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(16\times 9,24)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(16\times 9,24)</annotation></semantics></math> is transposed.</figcaption>
