<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every (unordered) quad array found in Table <a href="#S7.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is resolvable.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 8,14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 8,14)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(11\times 12,22)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(11\times 12,22)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays that are neither resolvable nor quad arrays, despite these parameters being admissible for resolvable triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">None of the <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(16\times 9,24)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>24</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(16\times 9,24)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays are quad arrays, despite the parameter set being admissible for quad arrays.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All 4 resolvable <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 8,14)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 8,14)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays have the same component designs: the column design is the unique resolvable 2-<math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(8,4,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(8,4,3)</annotation></semantics></math> design with unique resolution, and the row design is the 2-multiple of the symmetric 2-<math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,4,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,4,2)</annotation></semantics></math> design, the complement of the Fano plane.
Peculiarly, they come from pairwise non-isomorphic symmetric 2-designs in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, see Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table C.4 ‣ Appendix C Counts of extremal (unordered) triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, all 20 resolvable <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(11\times 12,22)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>22</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(11\times 12,22)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays have the same component designs: the column design is the unique resolvable 2-<math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(12,6,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(12,6,5)</annotation></semantics></math> design with unique resolution, and the row design is the 2-multiple of the unique symmetric 2-<math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(11,6,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(11,6,3)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
Again, they come from pairwise non-isomorphic symmetric 2-designs in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4 </span>Kirkman parades and resolvable <math id="S7.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Among the admissible non-extremal parameter sets, <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math> is the smallest one by any measure (number of rows, number of columns, number of symbols), and, as already mentioned, the only parameter set for which a non-extremal triple array has been found so far, see Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
It is admissible for resolvable triple arrays, with <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To generate all resolvable <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays, we apply the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for all possible combinations of a symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7,3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7,3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and a resolution <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
Up to isomorphism, there is only one choice for <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Fano plane <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resolutions of 2-<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> designs are known as <em id="S7.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kirkman parades</em>, named so by Cole <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">cole_kirkman_parades</span>]</cite>.
For a more contemporary treatment on Kirkman parades, see the recent work of Pavone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">pavoneSevenNonisomorphicSolutions2023</span>]</cite>.
There are 7 non-isomorphic Kirkman parades.
Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">pavoneSevenNonisomorphicSolutions2023</span>, Table 1]</cite>, we will refer to them as ‘1a’, ‘1b’, ‘7a’, ‘7b’, ‘19a’, ‘19b’ and ‘61’.
As the names suggest, they stem from four non-isomorphic resolvable 2-<math id="S7.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,3,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,3,1)</annotation></semantics></math> designs, three of which correspond to two Kirkman parades each, and one to a single Kirkman parade ‘61’.
The 7 Kirkman parades give rise to a total of 42 <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays and 85 <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays, see Table <a href="#S7.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for a summary.
An extended Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table A.1 ‣ Appendix A Counts of resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> is available in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Counts of resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
