<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kirkman parades ‘1a’ and ‘1b’ are the two non-isomorphic resolutions of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to the case <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=2</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 5.16. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>.
Kirkman parade ‘1b’ corresponds to the only previously known <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array from Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
This parade gives rise to 4 non-isomorphic <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays.
Two of these cannot be ordered, the third has two non-isotopic orderings, one of which is the triple array from Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and the fourth can be ordered uniquely up to isotopism, see Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table A.1 ‣ Appendix A Counts of resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure A.1 ‣ Appendix A Counts of resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kirkman parade ‘1a’, one of two resolutions of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponds to 4 non-isomorphic resolvable <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays, none of which can be ordered.
Moreover, in total, 12 out of 42 resolvable <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays cannot be ordered, with each Kirkman parade except ‘7a’ giving rise to at least one these 12, see Table <a href="#A1.T1" title="Table A.1 ‣ Appendix A Counts of resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.
This is in some sense in contrast with Conjectures <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 4.4 (Agrawal []). ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> and Observation <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Observation 7.2. ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that in the presentation given by Yucas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">yucas_structure_7x15</span>]</cite> on the structure of the <math id="S7.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array from Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the points in the blocks of the resolution had been ordered in such a way that the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> was in essence already solved, but no indication was given as to how to find this ordering.
Yucas remarked that there was ‘‘nothing special’’ about the choice of <math id="S7.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,2)</annotation></semantics></math> as the column design used in the construction of this triple array.
In view of Observations <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem6" title="Observation 7.6. ‣ 7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> and <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Observation 7.7. ‣ 7.4 Kirkman parades and resolvable (7×15,35)-triple arrays ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>, this remark is not quite accurate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.5 </span>Resolvable <math id="S7.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays</h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The parameter set <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math> is also admissible for resolvable triple arrays, with <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rrc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rrc}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
To produce a resolvable <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array, the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> requires a symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21,5,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>21</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21,5,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and a resolution <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,5,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,5,6)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
Up to isomorphism, the only choice for <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Galois projective plane <math id="S7.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(2,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(2,4)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resolutions of 2-<math id="S7.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(15,5,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(15,5,6)</annotation></semantics></math> designs have not been fully classified, but 149 non-isomorphic resolutions on these parameters have been constructed by Mathon and Rosa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mathon15FamilyBIBDs1989</span>, Table 6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each one of these 149 resolutions, we were able to produce a <math id="S7.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(21\times 15,63)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>21</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>63</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(21\times 15,63)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array by first applying the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with parallel classes of the resolution labeled in random order, and then finding some ordering of the produced unordered triple array using the Dancing cells algorithm.
An example is given in Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure B.1 ‣ Appendix B A (21×15,63)-triple array ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.
We emphasize that these are the first known triple arrays on this parameter set, and that this is just the second non-extremal parameter set on which triple arrays have been constructed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We did not attempt to fully enumerate resolvable triple arrays corresponding to resolutions from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">mathon15FamilyBIBDs1989</span>]</cite>.
Empirically, the number of non-isomorphic unordered triple arrays corresponding to each given resolution as well as the number of non-isotopic triple arrays corresponding to each produced unordered triple array seem to be very large, so full enumeration via the current approach does not seem feasible due to both space and time limitations.
Moreover, the currently known list of resolutions is likely not exhaustive, so neither would be the lists of corresponding (unordered) triple arrays.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.6 </span>Other non-extremal parameter sets</h3>

<div id="S7.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, all other non-extremal parameter sets <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> with known examples of resolvable 2-<math id="S7.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> designs are covered by Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 5.16. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a>.
The smallest such parameter set besides <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7\times 15,35)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>35</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7\times 15,35)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
We attempted to construct <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays by the same approach as in the previous section.
We obtained examples of <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays using the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with the symmetric 2-<math id="S7.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13,4,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13,4,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}:=\PG(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}:=\PG(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math> and two different resolutions <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of 2-<math id="S7.SS6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(40,4,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(40,4,1)</annotation></semantics></math> designs with randomly labeled blocks.
Both resolutions were produced by Sagemath <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">sagemath25</span>]</cite>, the first is a resolution of <math id="S7.SS6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,3)</annotation></semantics></math>, while the second comes from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bethDesignTheory1999</span>, Theorem VII.7.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unfortunately, we could not find an ordering of any such unordered triple array, despite the search running, in some cases, for multiple core-days before being aborted.
This does not necessarily imply that such an ordering does not exist, as we deemed that an exhaustive search for orderings of any given <math id="S7.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array would take an unreasonable amount of time.
It should be noted, however, that in all previously considered cases where the search was successful, at least some of the orderings were found almost immediately.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The complete list of 73343 non-isomorphic resolutions of <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PG(3,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PG(3,3)</annotation></semantics></math>, enumerated by Betten <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">BettenPackings</span>]</cite>, may prove useful in the search for <math id="S7.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays.
In view of the above though, it seems likely that some further theoretical guidance would be required for a computer search to successfully construct such a triple array.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Triple arrays and finite affine planes</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we further investigate extremal (unordered) triple arrays on the parameter set <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>, which we have already seen in the case <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math> of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem11" title="Theorem 5.11. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.11</span></a> and in Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Remark 5.12. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>.
We show that all such (unordered) triple arrays are resolvable, with the corresponding resolvable 2-design being an affine plane of order <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and propose two problems equivalent to the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for these parameter sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that Youden rectangles are triple arrays with <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=c</annotation></semantics></math>, or, equivalently, <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=e</annotation></semantics></math>.
Triple arrays with <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=e+1</annotation></semantics></math> are, in a sense, closest to Youden rectangles among all non-trivial triple arrays.
One can thus expect them to have simpler structure than triple arrays in general.
This is supported by the following theorem of Jäger, Markström, Shcherbak and the second author, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">jagerEnumerationConstructionRowColumn2025</span>, Theorem 3.2 and Remark 3.3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any binary equireplicate <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times c</annotation></semantics></math> row-column design with replication number <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=e+1</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies property <a href="#S2.I1.ix2" title="item (RR) ‣ Definition 2.5. ‣ 2.3 Triple arrays and component designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">(RR)</span></a> of triple arrays.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next lemma shows that (unordered) triple arrays with <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=e+1</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely <math id="S8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-(unordered) triple arrays with <math id="S8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=e</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,v)</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-trivial admissible parameter set with <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=e+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=e^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=e^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=e(e+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=e(e+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\frac{rc}{e}=\frac{(e+1)c}{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\frac{rc}{e}=\frac{(e+1)c}{e}</annotation></semantics></math>, so, due to <math id="S8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e+1</annotation></semantics></math> being coprime, <math id="S8.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> must be divisible by <math id="S8.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, <math id="S8.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}\equiv\frac{1(-1)}{-1}\equiv 1\pmod{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>≡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}\equiv\frac{1(-1)}{-1}\equiv 1\pmod{e}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A non-trivial parameter set has <math id="S8.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&lt;c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&lt;c</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S8.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}&lt;r=e+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=\frac{r(e-1)}{c-1}&lt;r=e+1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S8.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
This, in turn, implies <math id="S8.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\frac{r(e-1)}{\lambda_{cc}}+1=e^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\frac{r(e-1)}{\lambda_{cc}}+1=e^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\frac{rc}{e}=e(e+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\frac{rc}{e}=e(e+1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It turns out that all <math id="S8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-(unordered) triple arrays are resolvable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8.3</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any <math id="S8.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array is resolvable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array.
Its parameters are <math id="S8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=\lambda_{rc}=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=\lambda_{rc}=q</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{cc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{cc}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{rr}=q(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{rr}=q(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S8.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{1},\dots,R_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{1},\dots,R_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the row-sets and <math id="S8.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},\dots,C_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},\dots,C_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> the column-sets of <math id="S8.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, let <math id="S8.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the symbol set of <math id="S8.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>, and define <math id="S8.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}:=V\setminus R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}:=V\setminus R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S8.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of symbols missing from row <math id="S8.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each symbol appears in all rows but one, so the sets <math id="S8.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> form a partition of <math id="S8.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{i}|=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{i}|=q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The row design <math id="S8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-<math id="S8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1,q,q(q-1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1,q,q(q-1))</annotation></semantics></math> design, that is, the <math id="S8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-multiple of the trivial symmetric 2-<math id="S8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1,q,q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1,q,q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design.
The column design <math id="S8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is a 2-<math id="S8.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q^{2},q,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q^{2},q,1)</annotation></semantics></math> design, that is, a finite affine plane of order <math id="S8.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
The symbols of each set <math id="S8.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to <math id="S8.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> identical blocks in <math id="S8.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
On the other hand, for all <math id="S8.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,j</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S8.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{x}\cap C_{j}|=|C_{j}|-|R_{x}\cap C_{j}|=r-\lambda_{rc}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{x}\cap C_{j}|=|C_{j}|-|R_{x}\cap C_{j}|=r-\lambda_{rc}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so each set <math id="S8.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a parallel class of <math id="S8.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CD_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CD_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It follows that <math id="S8.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is resolvable.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.4</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 8.3. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a> implies that any <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array can be obtained as described in Remark <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem12" title="Remark 5.12. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.12</span></a>, that is, using the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> with the unique resolution of an affine plane of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> as one of the components.
On the other hand, by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, it can also be obtained using <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>.
In fact, both essentially describe the same process in two different ways.
For a finite projective plane <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, applying <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="In 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 4.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Agrawal’s construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> to its dual <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, which has lines of <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> as points, and points of <math id="S8.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> as blocks, corresponds to the classic construction of an affine plane by deleting a line from a projective plane.
The column-sets in both cases correspond to the points of the affine plane, and the row-sets correspond to the ‘points at infinity’, or, equivalently, to parallel classes of the affine lines, in the sense that each row-set ‘misses’ one parallel class.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 8.5</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a bijection between isomorphism classes of <math id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple arrays and isomorphism classes of affine planes of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, isomorphic unordered triple arrays have isomorphic column designs, so they do correspond to isomorphic affine planes.
On the other hand, the symmetric 2-design required for the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is a trivial 2-<math id="S8.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1,q,q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1,q,q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> design, thus the construction would always produce isomorphic unordered triple arrays, no matter how the parallel classes, blocks and symbols of the affine plane are labeled.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8.6</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an affine plane <math id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> the unique (up to isomorphism) resolvable <math id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array whose column design is isomorphic to <math id="S8.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We see that for parameter sets <math id="S8.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>, the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA construction problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> is equivalent to the question of existence of projective (or equivalently, affine) planes of order <math id="S8.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the remainder of this section, we present two equivalent reformulations of the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for these parameter sets that may prove to be easier to work with: one in terms of derangements, and one in terms of hypergraphs and partiteness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 8.7</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an affine plane of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with point set <math id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, label the parallel classes of its lines as <math id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Find a way to assign a permutation <math id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to each point <math id="S8.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
<ol id="S8.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">derangement</span> for any point <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p}(i)\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p}(i)\neq i</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S8.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">for any pair of distinct points <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,u\in P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,u\in P</annotation></semantics></math>, if the unique line through <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the parallel class <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S8.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p}(i)\neq\sigma_{u}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p}(i)\neq\sigma_{u}(i)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.8</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any affine plane <math id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> has a solution if and only if the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> has a solution for the <math id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="S8.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Label the points of <math id="S8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recalling the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, the symbols of <math id="S8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the lines of the plane, and, without loss of generality, the column-set <math id="S8.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the lines incident to the point <math id="S8.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the row-set <math id="S8.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the lines from all parallel classes but <math id="S8.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="S8.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> the unique line such that <math id="S8.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}\in\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)\in\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}\in\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)\in\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S8.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a solution to the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, that is, a triple array with <math id="S8.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}=U_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}=U_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for each point <math id="S8.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> define a permutation <math id="S8.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p_{j}}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p_{j}}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: for each parallel class <math id="S8.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S8.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p_{j}}(i)=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p_{j}}(i)=s</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S8.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the cell <math id="S8.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,j)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S8.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
These permutations form a solution to Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>: indeed, condition <a href="#S8.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> follows from the fact that <math id="S8.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}\cap\mathcal{L}_{i}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}\cap\mathcal{L}_{i}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, and condition <a href="#S8.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> holds because <math id="S8.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is binary, so no line appears two times in the same row.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, for a collection of permutations <math id="S8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p_{j}}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p_{j}}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> forming a solution to Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>, define an <math id="S8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times c</annotation></semantics></math> row-column design <math id="S8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> by placing <math id="S8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> in the cell <math id="S8.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma_{p_{j}}(i),j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma_{p_{j}}(i),j)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S8.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Repeating the arguments above in reverse, <math id="S8.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a triple array with <math id="S8.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}=U_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}=U_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p12.1" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to show by case analysis that there are no <math id="S8.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays.
This, however, gives no insights into why this parameter set is exceptional with regards to Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 4.4 (Agrawal []). ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.
In the next corollary, as an indication of the usefulness of the reformulation in Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>, we give an alternative proof of this non-existence fact using Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 8.8. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.8</span></a>, which highlights one reason why this parameter set is special.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 8.9</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There are no <math id="S8.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Combining Theorem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 8.3. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.3</span></a> with Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 8.8. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.8</span></a>, any <math id="S8.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3\times 4,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3\times 4,6)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array corresponds to a solution to Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> for <math id="S8.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, the affine plane of order 2.
Such a solution would include 4 pairwise different derangements from <math id="S8.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, however, there are only 2 derangements on 3 elements.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The next parameter set in the family <math id="S8.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S8.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4\times 9,12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4\times 9,12)</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to <math id="S8.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case, solving Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> requires 9 pairwise different derangements from <math id="S8.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and there are precisely that many derangements on 4 elements.
This provides some explanation for why the <math id="S8.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(4\times 9,12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(4\times 9,12)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array is unique up to isotopism, see Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p15.1" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S8.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> growing further, the number of derangements grows exponentially, quickly outpacing what is required for Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>, that is, the number of columns <math id="S8.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, there are 44 derangements vs. 16 columns for <math id="S8.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and 265 derangements vs. 25 columns for <math id="S8.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=5</annotation></semantics></math>.
This indicates that solving Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> should become easier as <math id="S8.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> grows, which is further supported by the fact that there are many non-isotopic <math id="S8.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(5\times 16,20)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(5\times 16,20)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple arrays (see Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).
Computationally we have found many solutions for the larger <math id="S8.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> we have tested, but we have not been able to find a direct description of a solution to Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> for general <math id="S8.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p16.1" class="ltx_p">To state the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> for parameter sets <math id="S8.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math> in yet another way, we need to introduce some terminology from hypergraph theory.
A <math id="S8.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S8.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-graph</span> <math id="S8.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> on a <span id="S8.p16.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vertex</span> set <math id="S8.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a collection of <math id="S8.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-subsets of <math id="S8.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> called <span id="S8.p16.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edges</span>.
It is <span id="S8.p16.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">linear</span> if any two distinct edges share at most one vertex.
It is <math id="S8.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S8.p16.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">partite</span> if one can partition the vertex set <math id="S8.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S8.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> parts such that each edge contains one vertex from each part.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 8.10</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For an affine plane of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> with points <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1},\dots,p_{q^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1},\dots,p_{q^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, label the parallel classes of its lines as <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{1},\dots,\mathcal{L}_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote by <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> the unique line such that <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}\in\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)\in\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}\in\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)\in\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define a <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: let</p>
<table id="S8.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V:=\{v_{ij}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1,1\leq j\leq q^{2}\}\cup\{w_{i}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V:=\{v_{ij}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1,1\leq j\leq q^{2}\}\cup\{w_{i}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">for each point <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> add to <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> the edge <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(p_{j}):=\{v_{ij}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(p_{j}):=\{v_{ij}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for each line <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\mathcal{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\mathcal{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> add to <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> the edge <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(l):=\{v_{ij}\,:\,l=\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)\}\cup\{w_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(l):=\{v_{ij}\,:\,l=\mathcal{L}_{i}(j)\}\cup\{w_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and, finally, add to <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> one more edge <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}:=\{w_{i}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}:=\{w_{i}\,:\,1\leq i\leq q+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Find a partition of <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+1</annotation></semantics></math> parts <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},V_{2},\ldots,V_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},V_{2},\ldots,V_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that any edge meets each <math id="S8.Thmtheorem10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in exactly one vertex.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p17.1" class="ltx_p">We note that <math id="S8.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S8.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1)q^{2}+(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1)q^{2}+(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and <math id="S8.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{2}+(q^{2}+q)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{2}+(q^{2}+q)+1</annotation></semantics></math> edges, and is linear.
If the partition sought in Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem10" title="Problem 8.10. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.10</span></a> can be found, this in other words means that the hypergraph <math id="S8.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S8.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-partite. We prove in the following proposition that Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem10" title="Problem 8.10. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.10</span></a> has a solution if and only if Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> does, and consequently, by Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 8.8. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.8</span></a>, if and only if
the <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="In 3 Unordered triple arrays ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 3.3</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(UTA ordering problem)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> does.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 8.11</span></span><span id="S8.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any affine plane of order <math id="S8.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem10" title="Problem 8.10. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.10</span></a> has a solution if and only if Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> has a solution.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose that <math id="S8.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S8.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-partite and let <math id="S8.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1},\dots,V_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1},\dots,V_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding vertex partition.
The edge <math id="S8.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has one vertex in each part, so, without loss of generality, we may assume by relabeling vertices that <math id="S8.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each point <math id="S8.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S8.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p_{j}}(i):=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p_{j}}(i):=s</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S8.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{ij}\in V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{ij}\in V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each edge <math id="S8.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(p_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(p_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> has one vertex in each part, so <math id="S8.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a permutation.
Each edge <math id="S8.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e(l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e(l)</annotation></semantics></math> has one vertex in each part, and <math id="S8.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies conditions <a href="#S8.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S8.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> from Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">Conversely, for a collection of permutations <math id="S8.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{p_{j}}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{p_{j}}\in\mathfrak{S}_{q+1}</annotation></semantics></math> forming a solution to Problem <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>, define the partition of the vertex set of <math id="S8.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S8.p19.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V_{s}:=\{v_{ij}\,:\,1\leq j\leq q^{2},\sigma_{p_{j}}(i)=s\}\cup\{w_{s}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{s}:=\{v_{ij}\,:\,1\leq j\leq q^{2},\sigma_{p_{j}}(i)=s\}\cup\{w_{s}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq s\leq q+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq s\leq q+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Repeating the arguments above in reverse, each edge of <math id="S8.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> contains one vertex from each of <math id="S8.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Concluding remarks</h2>

<section id="S9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.1 </span>No triple arrays via <math id="S9.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-resolutions</h3>

<div id="S9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a positive integer <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the following notions generalize parallel classes, resolutions and resolvable 2-designs.
An <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S9.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">parallel class</span> in a 2-design is a collection of blocks such that each point occurs in exactly <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of these blocks.
An <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S9.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolution</span> of a 2-design is a partition of the collection of blocks into <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-parallel classes.
A 2-design that admits an <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-resolution is called <math id="S9.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S9.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resolvable</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A potential generalization of the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> could use an <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-resolution <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> of a 2-<math id="S9.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c,v,r,e,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c,v,r,e,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design for some <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the number of <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-parallel classes in such an <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-resolution is <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{ve}{\alpha c}=\frac{r}{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{ve}{\alpha c}=\frac{r}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the construction to produce an unordered triple array, the second component would instead of a symmetric 2-<math id="S9.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,e,\lambda_{rrc})</annotation></semantics></math> design need to be a 2-design with <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> points and <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r}{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mi>α</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r}{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> blocks.
Unfortunately, due to <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="In 2.1 2-designs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fisher’s inequality)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a>, this is only possible when <math id="S9.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so this approach does not lead to any new unordered triple arrays.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">9.2 </span>Open questions</h3>

<div id="S9.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we reiterate the two versions of Agrawal’s conjecture on the existence of extremal triple arrays.
Note that our computational results (see Observation <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem2" title="Observation 7.2. ‣ 7.3 The extremal case ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>) provide some new evidence towards these conjectures.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjagrx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjagr">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjagrx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture </span><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 4.4 (Agrawal []). ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a></span><span id="Thmconjagrx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjagrx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjagrx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjagrx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If there is a symmetric 2-<math id="Thmconjagrx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r+c,r,\lambda_{cc})</annotation></semantics></math> design with <math id="Thmconjagrx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r-\lambda_{cc}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, then there is an <math id="Thmconjagrx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjagr2x1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjagr2">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjagr2x1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture </span><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Conjecture 4.8. ‣ 4 Agrawal’s construction ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a></span><span id="Thmconjagr2x1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-unordered triple array <math id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an <math id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r\times c,r+c-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r\times c,r+c-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array <math id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmconjagr2x1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{T}=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{T}=U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Resolving these conjectures in the special case of parameters <math id="S9.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math> amounts to solving either of the Problems <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> and <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem10" title="Problem 8.10. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.10</span></a>.
In particular, solving these problems for Galois affine planes would result in a new infinite series of triple arrays.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 9.1</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problems <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem7" title="Problem 8.7. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a> and <a href="#S8.Thmtheorem10" title="Problem 8.10. ‣ 8 Triple arrays and finite affine planes ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.10</span></a> have solutions for the Galois affine plane <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\AG(2,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>AG</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\AG(2,q)</annotation></semantics></math> for any prime order <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, there is a <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((q+1)\times q^{2},q(q+1))</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array for any prime power <math id="S9.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem16" title="Theorem 5.16. ‣ 5.3 Infinite families of resolvable unordered triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.16</span></a> provides an infinite family of non-extremal unordered triple arrays.
We conjecture that, for each parameter set in the family, at least some of these unordered triple arrays can be ordered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 9.2</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any prime power <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a <math id="S9.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{q^{3}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{4}-1}{q-1},\frac{(q^{4}-1)(q^{3}-1)}{(q^{2}-1)(q-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{q^{3}-1}{q-1},\frac{q^{4}-1}{q-1},\frac{(q^{4}-1)(q^{3}-1)}{(q^{2}-1)(q-1)})</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The next step towards this conjecture would be resolving the case <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=3</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to a <math id="S9.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array. It may well be that the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> can succeed, but, as discussed in Section <a href="#S7.SS6" title="7.6 Other non-extremal parameter sets ‣ 7 Enumeration and examples ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a>, other ideas may be needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 9.3</span></span><span id="S9.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Construct a <math id="S9.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(13\times 40,130)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>40,130</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(13\times 40,130)</annotation></semantics></math>-triple array.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since not all extremal triple arrays are resolvable, it would be natural to assume that the same holds in the non-extremal case.
However, the <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="In 5.1 Resolvable triple arrays ‣ 5 Triple arrays via resolutions ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_nameref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Construction 5.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RUTA construction)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></span></a> which is the only construction method currently available in this case, by definition only produces resolvable arrays.
Table <a href="#S9.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 9.2 Open questions ‣ 9 Concluding remarks ‣ Resolvable Triple Arrays" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> lists all non-extremal parameter sets with <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq 30</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S9.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\leq 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\leq 30</annotation></semantics></math> which are admissible for triple arrays but not for resolvable triple arrays.
Note that some of these parameter sets are admissible for quad arrays.</p>
</div>
